TP.register({id:"lp8-g5",lp:8,grade:"5",mode:"curriculum",checks:7,nextGrade:"6th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="The Swap Table";const FLU=["On the first Saturday in April the community center puts six folding tables out in the parking lot, and the seed swap opens at nine.","The rule is the same every year. Bring something, take something. Nobody counts, and nobody is turned away for arriving empty-handed, but almost everyone brings something anyway, because the whole point of the morning is that a packet of seed you grew yourself is worth more than a packet off a rack.","By ten the tables are a clutter of envelopes and jars and old film canisters, every one of them labeled in handwriting. Tomato, Cherokee Purple, saved 2024. Scarlet runner, from Aunt Vi, do not plant before May.","The best table is the far one, where the growers who have been doing this for thirty years sit in folding chairs and answer questions. You can hand one of them a seed you cannot name and get an answer in about four seconds. They will also tell you, without being asked, that you planted your squash too early last spring and that this is why it sat in the ground and did nothing until July.","Mr. Okafor runs that table, and he keeps a shoebox under his chair that he opens for almost nobody. Inside are the seeds he counts out rather than scoops: a bean his grandmother carried north in a coffee tin, a melon that will only set fruit in a hot dry August, and a lettuce he has been saving for eleven years because it refuses to turn bitter.","If you want one of those, you have to come back in the fall and tell him what happened to it. That is the real rule of the swap table, and nobody has ever written it down. You bring back the story, and the next spring the shoebox opens again."].join("\n\n");const STORY_TITLE="The Long Bed";const STORY=["The bed along the east fence was ninety feet long and never got the same weather twice. The north end lay in the shade of the maple until eleven. The south end took the sun from first light and dried out like a cracker by noon.","Thandi had argued for the long bed all winter. Everyone else wanted the square plots by the gate, which were flat and fair and dull.","“You know it is going to make you look bad,” Mr. Ferraro said. He had kept the plot next to hers for twenty years and he said what he thought. “Half your rows will fail.”","“That is the point,” she said.","She marked the bed into eighteen sections with string and planted the same six kinds of bean in each third of it. North, middle, south. Same seed, same day, same water.","By the middle of May the long bed looked like a badly knitted scarf. The north third was a thin green fuzz. The middle was thick as a hedge. The south third had three rows up and three rows bare.","Mr. Ferraro walked the whole length of it twice without saying anything, which for him was a speech.","“You could have grown twice as many beans in the square plots,” he said at last.","“I know.”","“But you would not have known anything.” He crouched at the south end and laid his hand flat on the soil, the way you would check a child for fever. “What is this one?”","“Kentucky Wonder. It did better down here than any of the others.”","He nodded slowly. Then he went back to his own plot, and the next morning Thandi found a row of stakes along his north edge, and a new string, and six small signs."].join("\n\n");const MICRO_TITLE="Why One Garden Has Many Climates";const MICRO=["Stand at one end of a garden and walk to the other and you cross more than one climate. Gardeners call the small pockets microclimates, and a garden the size of a classroom can hold half a dozen of them.","Three things make them. The first is sun. A bed on the south side of a wall can run ten degrees warmer than a bed six feet away in the wall's shadow, and warm soil is what a seed waits for. Most garden seeds will not start at all until the soil reaches about 45 degrees Fahrenheit, and some want 60.","The second is water. Water does not spread itself out evenly. It runs downhill, it pools in the low corner, and it is pulled out of the soil fastest where the wind reaches. A sandy bed can be dry two days after a storm while a clay bed twenty feet away is still soaked.","The third is shelter. A fence, a hedge, or a building slows the wind, and slower wind means less water lost from the leaves and less chill on a cold night. The sheltered side of a fence is measurably warmer at dawn than the open ground beyond it.","None of this is a problem to be fixed. A gardener who knows where the cold wet corner is plants the things that like it there. Half of growing well is matching the plant to the pocket."].join("\n\n");const microHtml=small=>F.passage(MICRO,MICRO_TITLE,!!small);const SRC_A_LINE="Source A: Soil Temperature, from Seed Starting Basics by Dr. Rosalind Achebe, 2022.";const SRC_A=["A seed is a clock that will not start until the soil is warm enough, and every kind has its own floor. Spinach will start at 40 degrees Fahrenheit. Corn will not start below 50. Peppers want 70 and sulk below it.","Warmth also sets the speed. Lettuce takes about fifteen days to come up at 40 degrees and about two days at 70.","Too warm will stop a seed as surely as too cold. Lettuce goes dormant above about 80 degrees, a habit that keeps it from sprouting into the middle of a hot summer."].join("\n\n");const SRC_B_LINE="Source B: Water and Air, from The Germination Handbook by Tomás Vega, 2021.";const SRC_B=["A dry seed is doing almost nothing. The first step of sprouting is water: the seed takes it in, swells, and the coat splits open.","After that the embryo begins to burn its stored food for energy, and burning takes oxygen. That is why soil packed hard, or left standing in water, will stop a seed that has all the water it could want. The air pockets between the grains of soil are what carry the oxygen in.","A seed sitting in a puddle has water on every side of it and no air at all, and it will rot before it sprouts."].join("\n\n");const SRC_C_LINE="Source C: Depth and Light, an extension bulletin by Hana Lindqvist, 2024.";const SRC_C=["Planting depth is a rule of thumb with one number in it. Bury a seed about two to three times as deep as the seed is wide. A bean as wide as a fingernail goes an inch down. A carrot seed the size of a peppercorn goes a quarter inch down or less.","Plant too deep and the shoot runs out of stored food before it reaches the light.","Some small seeds go further than that and need light itself before they will start. Lettuce and celery sprout poorly in the dark, so they are pressed onto the surface and barely covered at all."].join("\n\n");const threeTexts=()=>`<div class="texts" style="display:flex;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">`+[[SRC_A_LINE,SRC_A],[SRC_B_LINE,SRC_B],[SRC_C_LINE,SRC_C]].map(s=>`<article style="flex:1 1 280px"><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${s[0]}</p>${F.passage(s[1],"",true)}</article>`).join("")+`</div>`;const PLAN_READ=["The Spring Report from the Garden Committee.","Over the past two years the committee has mapped the garden into three zones and kept a separate record for each one. The zones are the strip along the south wall, the open middle, and the shade corner behind the shed.","This spring the committee planted the same radish seed in all three zones on the same morning, with the same water. The strip by the wall came up first. The open middle followed. The shade corner was last by a wide margin, and two of its six rows never came up at all.","The committee's recommendation is not to give up on the shade corner. It is to plant different things there. Lettuce and spinach both start in cool soil, and both turn bitter and go to seed once the weather warms, so the cool corner is exactly where they belong.","The committee asks every class to write its zone at the top of the planting chart from now on."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["germination","horticulture","photosynthesis","irrigation","perennial","microclimate","unpredictable","dormancy","cultivate","transplanting","thermometer","evaporation"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:16px;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.25rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">${t}</p>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.22rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;function noteBoxes(list){const bx=t=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;min-width:210px;min-height:110px;padding:8px 14px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0;font-size:1.25rem">${t}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure">${list.map(bx).join("")}</div>`}function areaModel(cols,rows,sc,sr,wLab,hLab){const SIDE=216,cw=SIDE/cols,ch=SIDE/rows,ox=96,oy=58,GW=SIDE,GH=SIDE,W=ox+GW+76,H=oy+GH+46;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A square one yard on each side, cut into ${cols} columns and ${rows} rows. The block ${sc} columns wide and ${sr} rows tall in the top left corner is shaded.">`;for(let r=0;r<rows;r++)for(let c=0;c<cols;c++)s+=`<rect x="${ox+c*cw}" y="${oy+r*ch}" width="${cw}" height="${ch}" fill="${c<sc&&r<sr?"var(--gold)":"var(--surface)"}" fill-opacity="${c<sc&&r<sr?".65":"1"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<rect x="${ox}" y="${oy}" width="${GW}" height="${GH}" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3.5"/>`;s+=`<path d="M${ox} ${oy-8} v-10 h${sc*cw} v10" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;s+=`<text x="${ox+sc*cw/2}" y="${oy-26}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${wLab}</text>`;s+=`<path d="M${ox-8} ${oy} h-10 v${sr*ch} h10" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;s+=`<text x="${ox-26}" y="${oy+sr*ch/2}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${hLab}</text>`;s+=`<text x="${ox+GW/2}" y="${oy+GH+32}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">1 yard</text>`;s+=`<text x="${ox+GW+10}" y="${oy+GH/2}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">1 yard</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Grower's Word Cards",short:"Word Cards",parts:[{std:["RF.5.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:80,dir:"Read each word out loud. Break it into parts you know, then put it back together.",desc:"reading unfamiliar multisyllabic words using syllable patterns and word parts",key:["Read each word in order: germination, horticulture, photosynthesis, irrigation, perennial, microclimate, unpredictable, dormancy, cultivate, transplanting, thermometer, evaporation.","Roots worth asking about: photo means light and synthesis means putting together (photosynthesis), hort means garden (horticulture), per means through and ann means year, so a perennial lives through the years, micro means small (microclimate), therm means heat and meter means measure (thermometer), dorm means sleep (dormancy), and dict means say inside unpredictable.","Affixes: un-, pre-, and -able all stack in unpredictable; -ation and -ion turn a verb into a noun in germination, irrigation, and evaporation; -ing in transplanting sits on top of trans- plus plant.","Mark a word wrong only if the student still cannot read it after one try at chunking it."]}]},{id:2,title:"Ten Times and One Tenth",short:"Place Value",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.1"],dur:140,dir:"Answer out loud. Say the value of the digit, not just its name.",desc:"how a digit's value changes from one place to the next in a multi-digit number",html:()=>lead("The rain gauge is read to the nearest thousandth of an inch, so the chart is full of decimals. Every place on that chart is worth ten times the place on its right.")+F.table(["Reading","What it was"],[["3.440 in","the big storm in March"],["55.500 in","rain for the whole of last year"],["2.682 in","the storm after it"],["7,777.700 in","a number the class wrote down to test the chart"]]),qs:["In the March storm reading 3.440, the 4 in the tenths place is worth how many times the 4 in the hundredths place?","In 55.500, what is the 5 in the ones place worth compared with the 5 in the tens place?","In 2.682, what is the value of the 8? Say it as a decimal and as a fraction.","One storm dropped 0.6 inches. A storm ten times bigger would drop how much?","A gauge reads 4 inches. One tenth of that reading is how much?","In 7,777.700, how many times greater is the 7 in the hundreds place than the 7 in the ones place?"],key:["10 times. The tenths place is 0.4 and the hundredths place is 0.04, and 0.04 × 10 = 0.4. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","One tenth as much. The ones 5 is worth 5 and the tens 5 is worth 50, and 50 ÷ 10 = 5. Partial: 10 times as much reverses the comparison and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","8 hundredths, which is 0.08, or 8/100. Partial: one of the decimal and the fraction given is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","6 inches. Moving a digit one place to the left multiplies its value by 10, so 0.6 × 10 = 6. Partial: 0.06 goes the wrong way along the places and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","0.4 inches. One tenth of 4 moves the 4 one place to the right, so 4 ÷ 10 = 0.4. Partial: 40 goes the wrong way along the places and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","100 times. The hundreds 7 is worth 700 and the ones 7 is worth 7, and 700 ÷ 7 = 100. Two places to the left is 10 × 10. Partial: 10 times counts only one place and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Read the Swap Table",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.5.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:180,dir:"Read the passage out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level passage read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy, then let the student read on to the end. In the record boxes put the words correct in the minute over the words the student reached in the minute, not over the whole passage. The passage is deliberately longer than a minute of reading so that the fastest readers are measured and not cut off: at the spring 90th percentile a grade 5 reader covers about 195 words in a minute, and this passage runs to __WC__.","Spring grade 5 benchmark: about 146 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, spring 50th percentile). 25th percentile is about 119 and 90th percentile is about 195. This is the last check of the year, so the spring column is the right one.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the two handwritten labels in the third paragraph, which should be read as labels and not as sentences; for the long sentence in the fourth paragraph that ends at July; and for the colon list of three seeds in the fifth paragraph, where a fluent reader groups each item and does not run them together."]}]},{id:4,title:"Many Climates, One Garden",short:"Microclimates",parts:[{std:["RI.5.1"],dur:130,dir:"Read the text. Then answer out loud. Quote the text word for word when you explain.",desc:"quoting accurately from an informational text when explaining it and when drawing inferences",html:()=>microHtml(true),qs:["What is a microclimate? Quote the text.","How many microclimates can a garden the size of a classroom hold? Quote the words.","What are the three things that make microclimates? Quote the sentence that names the first one.","How warm does soil have to be before most garden seeds will start? Quote the text.","The text never says that two of the same seed, planted in one garden on the same day, could come up a week apart. Use the text to explain why that could happen, and quote the words that support you."],key:["A small pocket of the garden with its own climate. Quotes “Gardeners call the small pockets microclimates.” (RI.5.1, quoting accurately)","Half a dozen. Quotes “a garden the size of a classroom can hold half a dozen of them.” (RI.5.1)","Sun, water, and shelter. Quotes “The first is sun,” and may add “The second is water” and “The third is shelter.” (RI.5.1)","About 45 degrees Fahrenheit, and some want 60. Quotes “Most garden seeds will not start at all until the soil reaches about 45 degrees Fahrenheit, and some want 60.” (RI.5.1)","Because the two spots are not at the same temperature or the same wetness, so one seed reaches its starting temperature days before the other. Quotes “A bed on the south side of a wall can run ten degrees warmer than a bed six feet away in the wall's shadow,” or “A sandy bed can be dry two days after a storm while a clay bed twenty feet away is still soaked.” (RI.5.1, quoting accurately to support an inference)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Unlike Pieces",short:"Add Fractions",parts:[{std:["5.NF.A.1"],dur:145,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the equivalent fractions you used.",desc:"adding and subtracting fractions and mixed numbers with unlike denominators",html:()=>lead("Every answer on this stop needs the two fractions written over the same denominator first."),qs:["2/3 + 1/5","7/8 − 1/6","1 1/4 + 2 2/3","4 1/6 − 1 3/4","3/10 + 1/4 + 2/5","Rafi writes 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5. Explain what is wrong with that and what the answer should be."],key:["13/15. In fifteenths, 2/3 = 10/15 and 1/5 = 3/15, and 10/15 + 3/15 = 13/15. Or: thirtieths work too, 20/30 + 6/30 = 26/30 = 13/15. Partial: 3/8 comes from adding across and does not count. (5.NF.A.1)","17/24. In twenty-fourths, 7/8 = 21/24 and 1/6 = 4/24, and 21/24 − 4/24 = 17/24. Partial: subtracting across, numerator from numerator and denominator from denominator, does not count. (5.NF.A.1)","3 11/12. As improper fractions, 1 1/4 = 5/4 = 15/12 and 2 2/3 = 8/3 = 32/12, and 15/12 + 32/12 = 47/12 = 3 11/12. Adding the whole numbers first and then 3/12 + 8/12 = 11/12 gives the same answer. Partial: 47/12 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.A.1, mixed numbers)","2 5/12. As improper fractions, 4 1/6 = 25/6 = 50/12 and 1 3/4 = 7/4 = 21/12, and 50/12 − 21/12 = 29/12 = 2 5/12. Or: regroup instead, 4 2/12 becomes 3 14/12, then take 1 9/12 away. Partial: 29/12 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.A.1, regrouping with mixed numbers)","19/20. In twentieths, 3/10 = 6/20, 1/4 = 5/20, and 2/5 = 8/20, and 6 + 5 + 8 = 19, so 19/20. Partial: two of the three renamed correctly is approaching. (5.NF.A.1, three addends)","Adding the tops and the bottoms treats halves and thirds as if they were the same size piece, and they are not. Both have to be rewritten in sixths first: 1/2 = 3/6 and 1/3 = 2/6, so the answer is 5/6. Notice that 2/5 is smaller than 1/2, and a sum cannot be smaller than one of the things added. Partial: naming the mistake with no correct answer given is approaching. (5.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:6,title:"The Long Bed",short:"Long Bed",parts:[{std:["RL.5.1"],dur:130,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Quote the story word for word when you explain.",desc:"quoting accurately from a story when explaining it and when drawing inferences",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["Why did Thandi want the long bed instead of the square plots? Quote the story.","What did Mr. Ferraro predict would happen? Quote it, and then quote the line that shows how close he was.","The story never says that the trial convinced Mr. Ferraro. How do you know it did? Quote the story.","What does Mr. Ferraro mean by “But you would not have known anything”? Quote the line it answers.","What kind of person is Mr. Ferraro? Quote two different parts of the story."],key:["Because the long bed is not the same all the way along, so it can answer a question the square plots cannot. Quotes “Everyone else wanted the square plots by the gate, which were flat and fair and dull,” and “‘That is the point,’ she said,” which she says right after he warns her that half her rows will fail. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately)","He predicted “Half your rows will fail.” The closest line is “The south third had three rows up and three rows bare,” which is exactly half of that third, and “The north third was a thin green fuzz” shows a weak result there as well. The middle third did not fail at all: “The middle was thick as a hedge.” (RL.5.1, inference with quotations)","Because he copied her design the next day. Quotes “the next morning Thandi found a row of stakes along his north edge, and a new string, and six small signs.” Six signs matches the six kinds of bean she planted in each third. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately to support an inference)","He means the square plots would have grown more beans but taught her nothing, and the information was worth more than the extra crop. It answers his own line, “You could have grown twice as many beans in the square plots.” (RL.5.1)","Blunt, experienced, and slow to show approval. Quotes “He had kept the plot next to hers for twenty years and he said what he thought,” and “Mr. Ferraro walked the whole length of it twice without saying anything, which for him was a speech,” or “He nodded slowly.” Needs two quotations. (RL.5.1)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Does That Answer Make Sense?",short:"Estimate",parts:[{std:["5.NF.A.2"],dur:145,dir:"For each one, say your estimate first, then work it out on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"fraction word problems with unlike denominators, with benchmark estimates to check the answer",html:()=>lead("Estimate before you compute. Compare each fraction to 0, to 1/2, and to 1, and say what the answer should be near."),qs:["A bed is 3/4 full of compost. The class adds another 1/8 of a bed. How full is it now? Estimate first.","The gauge held 1 1/2 inches after the first storm of the week and 3/5 of an inch after the second. What is the total for the two storms? Estimate first.","A seed packet holds 2/3 of a cup. The class uses 1/4 of a cup. How much is left?","Sol says 2/5 + 1/2 = 3/7. Use benchmarks to show that answer cannot be right, then give the correct one.","Three rows need 1/2 of a bag of soil, 1/3 of a bag, and 1/4 of a bag. Is one bag enough? Show the total.","The tank was 5/6 full before watering and 1/3 full after. What fraction of a tank was used?"],key:["7/8 of a bed. Estimate: 3/4 is near 1 and 1/8 is small, so the answer should be just under 1. In eighths, 3/4 = 6/8, and 6/8 + 1/8 = 7/8. Partial: 7/8 right with no estimate made first is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","2 1/10 inches. Estimate: 1 1/2 plus a bit over 1/2 should be a little over 2. In tenths, 1 1/2 = 15/10 and 3/5 = 6/10, and 15/10 + 6/10 = 21/10 = 2 1/10. Partial: 21/10 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","5/12 of a cup. In twelfths, 2/3 = 8/12 and 1/4 = 3/12, and 8/12 − 3/12 = 5/12. Estimate: a bit under 1/2 a cup. Partial: 5/12 right with no estimate is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","1/2 by itself is already 1/2, and 3/7 is less than 1/2 because half of 7 is 3 1/2. A sum cannot be smaller than one of the numbers added, so 3/7 is impossible. Sol added the tops and the bottoms. In tenths, 2/5 = 4/10 and 1/2 = 5/10, so the answer is 9/10. Partial: showing it is wrong with no correct answer given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, assessing reasonableness with benchmarks)","No, one bag is not enough. In twelfths, 1/2 = 6/12, 1/3 = 4/12, and 1/4 = 3/12, and 6 + 4 + 3 = 13, so the total is 13/12 = 1 1/12 bags. Estimate: 1/2 plus 1/3 is already more than 3/4, so adding 1/4 was always going to pass 1. Partial: saying no with no total shown is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","1/2 of a tank. In sixths, 1/3 = 2/6, and 5/6 − 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2. Partial: 3/6 right with no simpler form given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)"]}]},{id:8,title:"A Badly Knitted Scarf",short:"Word Pictures",parts:[{std:["RL.5.4"],dur:130,dir:"Read the story again. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases in a story, including similes and metaphors",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["The south end “dried out like a cracker by noon.” What kind of figurative language is that, and what does it tell you about that soil?","“The long bed looked like a badly knitted scarf.” What does the comparison make you picture?","“The north third was a thin green fuzz. The middle was thick as a hedge.” One of those is a metaphor and one is a simile. Which is which, and what does each one show?","Mr. Ferraro “walked the whole length of it twice without saying anything, which for him was a speech.” What does that last phrase mean?","He “laid his hand flat on the soil, the way you would check a child for fever.” What does that comparison tell you about how he treats the soil?"],key:["A simile, because it compares with the word like. It tells you the south soil goes hard, dry, and crumbly very fast in full sun, and that it does it by the middle of the day. (RL.5.4, simile)","An uneven stretch with thick parts, thin parts, and holes running one after another along its length, the way dropped stitches show in a scarf. It is a simile for the whole ninety feet, not for one section. (RL.5.4, simile)","“A thin green fuzz” is a metaphor: it calls the sprouts a fuzz outright, without like or as, and shows growth so short and sparse that it reads as a haze of green rather than rows. “Thick as a hedge” is a simile, signaled by as, and shows the middle so full that you cannot see through it. Put together they measure the difference between the zones. (RL.5.4, metaphor and simile)","He is a man who says very little, so two slow passes in silence was a long and serious comment from him. It tells you he was taking the trial seriously, not dismissing it. (RL.5.4, the meaning of a phrase as used in the text)","He handles the soil carefully and with attention, the way you touch someone to find something out rather than to move them. He is checking the heat and the dryness, and the comparison makes the bed something he is looking after. (RL.5.4, figurative language)"]}]},{id:9,title:"A Fraction of a Fraction",short:"Multiply",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.4"],dur:155,dir:"Use the picture for number 4. Work the rest on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"multiplying a fraction or whole number by a fraction, including the area of a rectangle with fractional sides",html:()=>lead("The square below is one square yard, cut into fourths across and thirds down. The shaded block is the corner the class planted."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${group(areaModel(4,3,3,2,"3/4 yd","2/3 yd"),"The planted corner")}</div>`,qs:["2/3 × 4/5","3/4 × 12","A tank is 3/4 full. The class uses 2/5 of what is in it. What fraction of a full tank did they use?","Look at the picture. The shaded block is 3/4 of a yard by 2/3 of a yard. What is its area in square yards? Use the small rectangles in the picture to show why.","A raised bed is 2 1/2 feet by 1 1/2 feet. What is its area?","A row is 7/8 of a meter long. The class plants 3/4 of that row. What fraction of a meter is planted?"],key:["8/15. Multiply the tops and multiply the bottoms: (2 × 4)/(3 × 5) = 8/15. Or: an area model of 15 parts with 8 of them shaded. (5.NF.B.4a)","9. 3/4 × 12 = 36/4 = 9. Also 12 ÷ 4 = 3, and 3 × 3 = 9. Partial: 36/4 left unsimplified is approaching. (5.NF.B.4a, a whole number times a fraction)","3/10 of a full tank. 2/5 × 3/4 = 6/20 = 3/10. Or: an area model of 20 parts with 6 of them shaded. (5.NF.B.4a, a fraction of a fraction)","1/2 of a square yard. Cutting the square into 4 columns and 3 rows makes 12 small rectangles, and each one is 1/12 of a square yard. The shaded block covers 3 columns by 2 rows, which is 6 of them, and 6/12 = 1/2. In numbers, 3/4 × 2/3 = 6/12 = 1/2. Partial: 6/12 right with no simpler form, or with no use of the picture, is approaching. (5.NF.B.4b, area by tiling with unit fraction pieces)","3 3/4 square feet. 2 1/2 = 5/2 and 1 1/2 = 3/2, and (5 × 3)/(2 × 2) = 15/4 = 3 3/4. Or: split it into 2 × 1 1/2 = 3 and 1/2 × 1 1/2 = 3/4, then add. (5.NF.B.4b, fractional side lengths)","21/32 of a meter. 3/4 × 7/8 = 21/32. Or: an area model of 32 parts with 21 of them shaded. (5.NF.B.4a)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Three Reasons a Seed Sits Still",short:"Three Sources",parts:[{std:["RI.5.9"],dur:150,dir:"Read all three sources. They are about the same subject. Then answer out loud, using more than one source in every answer.",desc:"putting several texts on one subject together in order to speak about the subject knowledgeably",html:threeTexts,qs:["Using all three sources, say everything a seed needs before it will sprout.","A class plants lettuce in a hot greenhouse in July, half an inch deep, in soil that stays soaked. Use the three sources to name three separate reasons it may not come up. Say which source each reason comes from.","Source A says warmth also sets the speed. Use its numbers to say how much sooner lettuce comes up at 70 degrees than at 40.","Each source names a way a seed can fail even when the gardener did everything else right. Name one from each source.","Put the three sources together and explain to a third grader, in two or three sentences, what a seed needs."],key:["Warm enough soil, and not too warm, with each kind having its own floor (Source A); water taken in through the coat, and air in the soil to supply oxygen (Source B); the right planting depth, about two to three times the seed's width, and light for the small seeds that need it (Source C). Needs facts from all three. (RI.5.9, integrating several texts)","Too warm: lettuce goes dormant above about 80 degrees (Source A). No air: soil left standing in water has no oxygen, and the seed rots before it sprouts (Source B). Too deep and too dark: a carrot-sized seed goes a quarter inch or less, and lettuce sprouts poorly in the dark, so half an inch buries it (Source C). Needs all three, with the source named. (RI.5.9)","About thirteen days sooner, because lettuce takes about fifteen days at 40 degrees and about two days at 70. That is roughly seven times faster. (RI.5.9, using a source's numbers)","Source A: the soil is at the wrong temperature for that kind of seed, too cold or too hot. Source B: the soil is packed hard or waterlogged, so there is no oxygen. Source C: the seed is buried deeper than two to three times its width, or a light-needing seed is covered. (RI.5.9, a point from each text)","Any clear two or three sentences that pull from all three sources, such as: a seed needs soil that is warm but not hot, water to swell it and air to breathe, and to sit at the right depth, which is shallow for a small seed. Mark it down if only one source is used. (RI.5.9, speaking knowledgeably)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Filling the Beds",short:"Volume",parts:[{title:"Counting Cubes",std:["5.MD.C.3"],dur:110,dir:"Use the picture. Answer out loud and say the unit every time.",desc:"understanding volume as the number of unit cubes that fill a solid with no gaps or overlaps",html:()=>lead("The model below is built from cubes that measure 1 foot on every edge. One of those cubes is called a unit cube."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${group(F.prism(4,3,2),"A model of the tool crate, built from 1 foot cubes")}</div>`,qs:["What is a unit cube, and how much volume does one of them have?","How many cubes are in one layer of the model, and how many layers are there?","What is the volume of the whole model?","A different solid is packed with 30 of these cubes and another is packed with 18. Which has the greater volume, and by how much?","A box is filled with 1 inch cubes, but they are stacked loosely with gaps between them. Can you find the volume by counting the cubes? Tell why or why not."],key:["A cube that measures 1 unit on every edge. It has a volume of 1 cubic unit, and here that is 1 cubic foot. Partial: defining the cube with no volume given is approaching. (5.MD.C.3a)","12 cubes in one layer, because the base is 4 cubes by 3 cubes, and there are 2 layers. Partial: one of the layer count and the number of layers is approaching. (5.MD.C.3b)","24 cubic feet, because 12 cubes in a layer and 2 layers makes 24 unit cubes, and each one is 1 cubic foot. Partial: 24 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.3b)","The one packed with 30 cubes, by 12 cubic inches worth of cubes. Volume is counted in unit cubes, so 30 cubes is 30 cubic units and 18 cubes is 18 cubic units. Partial: naming the bigger one with no difference given is approaching. (5.MD.C.3b)","No. Counting cubes only gives the volume when the solid is packed with no gaps and no overlaps. With gaps, the cubes do not fill the space, so the count is less than the real volume. Partial: saying no with nothing said about gaps and overlaps is approaching. (5.MD.C.3b)"]},{title:"Soil to Order",std:["5.MD.C.5"],dur:150,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",desc:"finding volume with length times width times height, with base area times height, and for a solid made of two boxes",html:()=>lead("The long bed is being rebuilt out of two rectangular boxes that sit end to end and do not overlap."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:28px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.prism(8,3,1),"Box 1: 8 ft by 3 ft by 1 ft")}${group(F.prism(4,3,1),"Box 2: 4 ft by 3 ft by 1 ft")}</div>`,qs:["A seed box is 6 cm by 4 cm by 3 cm. What is its volume?","A planter has a base area of 35 square feet and is 4 feet deep. What is its volume?","A planter holds 90 cubic feet and its base area is 15 square feet. How deep is it?","Look at the two boxes. How much soil does the whole rebuilt bed need?","Soil comes in bags of 2 cubic feet. How many bags does the rebuilt bed take?","A storage crate is 12 inches by 9 inches by 8 inches. What is its volume in cubic inches?"],key:["72 cubic centimeters. 6 × 4 × 3 = 72. Or: 6 × 4 = 24 for the base, then 24 × 3. (5.MD.C.5a)","140 cubic feet. Volume is the base area times the height, so 35 × 4 = 140. Partial: 140 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.5b)","6 feet deep. 90 ÷ 15 = 6. Or: try heights until 15 × h = 90, which lands on 6. (5.MD.C.5b, working backward from V = B × h)","36 cubic feet. Box 1 is 8 × 3 × 1 = 24 and Box 2 is 4 × 3 × 1 = 12, and 24 + 12 = 36. Volume adds when a solid is split into boxes that do not overlap. Or: the two together make one 12 by 3 by 1 box, 36. Partial: one of the two boxes worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5c)","18 bags. 36 ÷ 2 = 18. Partial: the division set up right on a wrong volume still earns credit here; the method is what is rated. (5.MD.C.5c)","864 cubic inches. 12 × 9 = 108, and 108 × 8 = 864. Or: 9 × 8 = 72 for one face, then 72 × 12. (5.MD.C.5a)"]}]},{id:12,title:"The Garden Map",short:"Map Grid",parts:[{std:["5.G.A.1"],dur:130,dir:"Use the map. Answer out loud. Say both numbers of an ordered pair in the right order.",desc:"the coordinate system: axes, the origin, and reading an ordered pair as travel along each axis",html:()=>lead("The garden map puts the gate at the corner where the two lines cross. B is the bean bed, C is the carrot bed, K is the compost bin, and T is the water tap. Each square is one step."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.coordGrid({max:10,size:400,points:[[2,9,"B"],[2,5,"C"],[6,3,"K"],[9,2,"T"]]})}</div>`,qs:["Write the ordered pair for the compost bin, K.","What are the two lines through the gate called, and what is the gate point itself called?","Explain how to travel from the gate to the water tap. The tap is T on the map. One number of the pair tells you how far to go first. Which number is it, and along which line?","Look at B and C. What do their ordered pairs have in common, and how many steps apart are they?","Why is the point (3, 7) not the same place as the point (7, 3)?","A new bed goes 4 steps along from the gate and 8 steps up. Write its ordered pair, and say where it sits compared with B."],key:["(6, 3). Partial: (3, 6) reverses the pair and is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","The horizontal line is the x-axis and the vertical line is the y-axis. The point where they meet is the origin, written (0, 0). Partial: two of the three names given is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","Go 9 steps along the x-axis from the origin, then 2 steps up parallel to the y-axis. The first number of the pair is the x-coordinate and it always gives the travel along the x-axis. Partial: the route right with nothing said about which number comes first is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","They have the same first number, 2, so both beds are 2 steps along the x-axis from the origin and sit on the same vertical line. They are 4 steps apart, because 9 − 5 = 4. Partial: naming the shared first number with no distance given is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","Because the order of the two numbers is part of the name of the point. In (3, 7) you travel 3 along the x-axis and 7 up; in (7, 3) you travel 7 along and 3 up. Those land in different places. Partial: saying they are different with no reason from the order is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","(4, 8). It is 2 steps to the right of B and 1 step below B, because B is at (2, 9). Partial: the pair right with no comparison to B is approaching. (5.G.A.1)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Fix the Trial Log",short:"Fix It",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.5.1"],dur:80,dir:"Each line from the trial log has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"fixing spoken grammar mistakes (perfect tenses, tense shifts, correlative conjunctions)",qs:["By the time the bell rang, Thandi counts eighteen sections.","Neither the beans or the peas came up in the shade corner.","We planted the seeds and then we water them every morning.","Mr. Ferraro has checked the soil yesterday.","By next June the class will finish the whole trial."],key:["By the time the bell rang, Thandi had counted eighteen sections. The counting finished before the bell, so it takes the past perfect. (L.5.1b, perfect verb tenses)","Neither the beans nor the peas came up in the shade corner. Neither always pairs with nor. (L.5.1e, correlative conjunctions)","We planted the seeds and then we watered them every morning. Both verbs have to stay in the past. (L.5.1d, correcting a shift in tense)","Mr. Ferraro checked the soil yesterday. A finished time such as yesterday takes the simple past, not the present perfect. (L.5.1d, inappropriate shift in verb tense)","By next June the class will have finished the whole trial. An action completed before a future time takes the future perfect. (L.5.1b, perfect verb tenses)"]},{title:"Build a Line",std:["L.5.1"],dur:60,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"spoken grammar items (conjunctions, correlative pairs, tense meaning, prepositions and interjections)",qs:["Name the conjunction in this sentence and say what it does: Thandi planted the long bed although everyone told her not to.","Use the pair not only and but also in one sentence about the garden.","Name the tense and say what it tells you: She had planted the same seed in all three zones before the rain came.","In this sentence, name the interjection and name one preposition: Wow, the beans in the middle third grew right through the string."],key:["although, and it is a subordinating conjunction. It joins a contrast to the main clause and shows she planted the bed in spite of the advice. (L.5.1a, conjunctions and their function)","Any correct sentence with both halves of the pair, such as: Not only did the middle third come up, but it also grew thicker than the hedge. The pair has to join two parts of equal weight. (L.5.1e, correlative conjunctions)","Past perfect. It tells you the planting was already finished before the rain arrived, so it puts the two past events in order. (L.5.1c, verb tense to convey sequence)","Interjection: Wow. Prepositions: in, or through. (L.5.1a, prepositions and interjections)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Say the Report Back",short:"Summarize",parts:[{std:["SL.5.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:130,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then summarize out loud. For the last two, use the graph.",desc:"summarizing a text read aloud and summarizing information shown on a graph",read:PLAN_READ,html:()=>lead("Your teacher will read you the garden committee's spring report. Listen for the main points. Then summarize it in your own words, and after that look at the graph."),fig:()=>F.row([F.barGraph({title:"Days to the first sprout",cats:["Wall","Middle","Shade"],vals:[6,9,15],max:16,step:2}),"Wall strip, open middle, shade corner. The same radish seed in all three, planted on one morning."]),qs:["Summarize the report in two or three sentences.","What did the committee recommend, and what reason did it give? Summarize both.","What did the committee ask every class to do from now on?","Summarize what the graph shows.","Which zone took the longest to sprout, and how many more days than the fastest zone?"],key:["The committee split the garden into three zones, planted the same radish seed in all three on one morning, and found that they did not behave the same: the wall strip came up first, the middle next, and the shade corner last by a wide margin, with two of its six rows not coming up at all. A summary, not a retelling of every sentence. (SL.5.2, summarizing a text read aloud)","Recommendation: do not give up on the shade corner, plant different things there instead. Reason: lettuce and spinach start in cool soil and turn bitter and go to seed once it warms up, so a cool corner suits them. Both parts needed. (SL.5.2)","Write the zone at the top of the planting chart. (SL.5.2, a supporting detail)","The graph shows how many days each zone took to produce its first sprout from the same seed planted on the same day. The wall was fastest and the shade corner was slowest, and the gap between them is large. (SL.5.2, information presented visually and quantitatively)","The shade corner, at 15 days. The fastest was by the wall at 6 days, so the shade corner took 9 more days. (SL.5.2)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Three Sources, Three Boxes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.5.8"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:115,dir:"Read the three sources. Take your notes on your paper in your own words, then list your sources underneath. Hold it up when you finish.",desc:"summarizing or paraphrasing information from several sources in notes, and listing the sources",html:()=>`${threeTexts()}<p class="big">Make three boxes on your paper, labeled like the ones below. Write at least two notes in each box, in your own words. Under the boxes, write a list of your three sources.</p>${noteBoxes(["Warmth","Water and air","Depth and light"])}<p class="small" style="text-align:center;font-size:1.2rem">A note is short. Summarize or put it in your own words rather than copying a sentence out.</p>`,key:["The Warmth box has at least two notes from Source A, such as every kind has its own starting temperature, spinach 40 and corn 50 and peppers 70, warmth also sets the speed, lettuce stops above about 80. (W.5.8, gathering relevant information)","The Water and air box has at least two notes from Source B, such as the seed takes in water and swells until the coat splits, the embryo burns stored food and needs oxygen, packed or flooded soil has no air pockets, a seed in a puddle rots. (W.5.8)","The Depth and light box has at least two notes from Source C, such as plant two to three times as deep as the seed is wide, a bean an inch down and a carrot seed a quarter inch or less, too deep and the shoot runs out of food, lettuce and celery need light so they sit on the surface. (W.5.8)","The notes are summarized or paraphrased rather than copied word for word out of the sources. A note that matches a source sentence exactly does not meet this check. (W.5.8, summarize or paraphrase in notes)","A source list with all three, each naming a title and an author: Soil Temperature by Dr. Rosalind Achebe, Water and Air by Tomás Vega, and Depth and Light by Hana Lindqvist. (W.5.8, provide a list of sources)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Speak to the Committee",short:"Speak Up",parts:[{std:["SL.5.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:135,dir:"Read the table and plan for 1 minute. Then speak for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken opinion, sequenced logically, with facts and descriptive details from a table",html:()=>lead("A cold frame is a low box with a clear lid that traps the sun's heat and warms the soil inside it. The committee has enough money for one cold frame and three zones to choose from.")+F.table(["This spring","By the wall","Open middle","Shade corner"],[["Hours of direct sun in April","7","5","2"],["Soil at 9 a.m. on April 15","62 °F","55 °F","48 °F"],["Days to the first radish sprout","6","9","15"],["Square feet free for the frame","90","240","60"],["After a storm","dry in 1 day","dry in 2 days","wet for 4 days"]])+`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Tell the committee which zone should get the cold frame. Give your reasons in an order that builds, use facts from the table, and add a detail that helps them picture the spot.</p>`+pills("My choice","My first reason","My second reason","What someone might say back","What I want the committee to do"),key:["States one clear choice and keeps to it for the whole talk. Any of the three zones can be argued. The wall case rests on 7 hours of sun and soil already at 62 degrees, so a frame there would get warmest. The shade corner case rests on 2 hours of sun, 48 degree soil, and 15 days to sprout, so it needs the help most. (SL.5.4, presenting an opinion)","Sequences the ideas logically, so each reason follows the one before instead of jumping around (SL.5.4)","Uses appropriate facts from the table, stated accurately. A changed number does not count. (SL.5.4, appropriate facts)","Adds at least one relevant descriptive detail that is not a number, such as the puddle that sits in the shade corner for four days after a storm, or how early the sun reaches the wall. (SL.5.4, relevant descriptive details)","Speaks clearly at an understandable pace, loud enough to hear, for 1 to 2 minutes (SL.5.4)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Garden Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"The trial bed or the square plots?"},std:["W.5.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>`<p class="big">The garden has room for the long trial bed or for the square plots by the gate, but not for both. Write to the garden committee and tell them which one to keep. Use the fact box. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: two ways to use the ground",["<strong>The long bed.</strong> Ninety feet along the east fence, marked into 18 sections across 3 zones. This spring 11 of the 18 sections came up well enough to crop. The trial is the only reason anyone knows that Kentucky Wonder is the one bean that will come up at the hot south end.","<strong>The square plots.</strong> Twelve even plots by the gate, all flat and all in full sun. Every one of the twelve crops every year, and together they grow about twice as many beans as the long bed does.","No other garden in the county is running a bean trial.","The food from the garden goes to the community kitchen."])}${checklist(["Open by naming the topic and stating your opinion clearly","Put your reasons in an order that builds, with the strongest one placed on purpose","Back every reason with a fact from the box, used accurately","Link your opinion and your reasons with phrases such as because, in addition, for this reason, on the other hand","End with a conclusion that follows from the reasons you gave"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states an opinion clearly (W.5.1a)","Creates an organizational structure in which the ideas are logically grouped and support the writer's purpose (W.5.1a)","Supports the opinion with facts and details from the box, used accurately, such as 11 of 18 sections, twice as many beans, or the Kentucky Wonder finding (W.5.1b)","Links opinion and reasons with words, phrases, and clauses such as consequently, in addition, for this reason, on the other hand (W.5.1c)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from the opinion presented (W.5.1d)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Why one garden has many climates"},std:["W.5.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to teach a new gardener why one garden can hold more than one climate, and what that means for where things get planted. Use facts from the text. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Open by naming the topic and giving the reader a general idea of what is coming","Group what you say so that each cause has its own stretch of writing","Use facts, definitions, concrete details, and at least one number from the text","Link ideas with words such as because, for example, as a result, in contrast","Use the words a gardener would use and tell the reader what they mean","End with a closing section that follows from the information"])}${lookBack("the text",microHtml(true))}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and gives the reader a general observation of what is coming (W.5.2a)","Groups related information logically, with each cause developed on its own (W.5.2a)","Develops the topic with facts, definitions, concrete details, and at least one number used accurately, such as ten degrees warmer, 45 degrees Fahrenheit, or half a dozen microclimates (W.5.2b)","Links ideas within and across paragraphs with words, phrases, and clauses such as because, for example, as a result, in contrast (W.5.2c)","Uses precise language and domain-specific vocabulary, such as microclimate or shelter, and ends with a concluding section (W.5.2d and W.5.2e)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"🌱",hint:"The morning the north third came up"},std:["W.5.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You have been watching one bed all spring and it has done nothing. This morning you get there before anybody else, and something has changed. Write the story of that morning. Write 10 to 12 sentences.</p>${pills("At first","Then","After that","By the time","In the end")}${checklist(["Set up the situation and introduce the narrator and anyone else in the story","Tell the events in an order that unfolds naturally","Use dialogue, description, and pacing to show how people respond","Use transition words and phrases to manage the order of events","Use precise words and sensory detail","End with something that follows from the events"])}`,key:["Orients the reader by establishing a situation and introducing a narrator and characters (W.5.3a)","Organizes an event sequence that unfolds naturally (W.5.3a)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, description, and pacing to develop experiences and responses (W.5.3b)","Uses a variety of transitional words, phrases, and clauses to manage the sequence of events (W.5.3c)","Uses concrete words, phrases, and sensory details, and ends with a conclusion that follows from the narrated experiences (W.5.3d and W.5.3e)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Inside the Brackets",short:"Brackets",parts:[{std:["5.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work each one on your paper. Say the value out loud.",desc:"evaluating numerical expressions with parentheses and brackets, and placing grouping symbols to make an expression true",html:()=>lead("The crew writes its planting totals as expressions so the order of the work is plain to read."),qs:["Evaluate 3 x (14 + 6) - 12.","Evaluate 2 x [15 + (9 - 4)].","Put one pair of parentheses into 24 - 6 x 3 + 2 so that it equals 56. Where do they go?"],key:["48. Inside the parentheses first: 14 + 6 = 20. Then 3 x 20 = 60, and 60 - 12 = 48. A student who works left to right gets 3 x 14 = 42 and lands somewhere else. Partial: 60 found by stopping before the subtraction is approaching.","40. The innermost group first: 9 - 4 = 5. Then 15 + 5 = 20 inside the brackets, and 2 x 20 = 40. Partial: 20 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching.","(24 - 6) x 3 + 2 = 56. 24 - 6 = 18, then 18 x 3 = 54, then 54 + 2 = 56. Any other placement misses: without the parentheses the expression is 24 - 18 + 2 = 8. Partial: parentheses placed anywhere that does not give 56 does not count."]}]},{id:19,title:"Read the Seed Weights",short:"Thousandths",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.3"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the table. Write your answers on your paper and say them out loud.",desc:"reading, writing and comparing decimals to thousandths",html:()=>lead("The seed shop weighs every packet on a scale that reads to the nearest thousandth of a gram."),fig:()=>F.table(["Packet","Weight in grams"],[["sunflower","4.075"],["pea","4.57"],["radish","4.507"],["basil","4.5"]]),qs:["Read the sunflower weight out loud. Then write the radish weight in words.","Write the pea weight in expanded form.","Put all four weights in order from least to greatest. Then compare the basil weight and the radish weight with the symbol > or <."],key:["Four and seventy-five thousandths. The radish weight in words is four and five hundred seven thousandths. Reading 4.075 as four point zero seven five is a description of the digits, not the number name, so ask again for the place name. Partial: one of the two weights put into words is approaching.","4.57 is 4 + 0.5 + 0.07. Writing it as (4 x 1) + (5 x 1/10) + (7 x 1/100) is correct too. Partial: one of the two places written is approaching.","4.075, 4.5, 4.507, 4.57. And 4.5 < 4.507, which can also be written 4.507 > 4.5. The trap is 4.075: a student who ignores the zero reads it as the largest instead of the smallest. The other trap is 4.5 against 4.507, where the longer string of digits is not the bigger number. Partial: the order right with the two weights not compared is approaching."]}]},{id:20,title:"Share It Out",short:"Share It",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.3"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Work each one on your paper. Say your answer out loud.",desc:"reading a fraction as division and solving sharing problems with mixed number answers",html:()=>lead("Everything that comes into the shed gets split evenly between the beds."),qs:["What division does the fraction 3/4 stand for? Say what the answer means when 3 whole trays of soil are shared equally among 4 beds.","Nine people share a 50 pound sack of seed potatoes equally by weight. How many pounds does each person get? Between which two whole numbers does your answer lie?","Seven bags of compost are shared equally among 4 beds. Write that as a division and give the answer as a mixed number."],key:["3 / 4, that is 3 divided by 4. Each bed gets 3/4 of a tray. Check it the other way: 4 beds with 3/4 of a tray each is 4 x 3/4 = 3 trays, which is what there was to share. Partial: the division right with nothing said about what each bed gets is approaching.","50 / 9 = 5 and 5/9 pounds each. It lies between 5 and 6, because 9 x 5 = 45 and 9 x 6 = 54, and 50 sits between them. An answer of 5 remainder 5 is not finished: the leftover has to be shared too. Partial: 5 remainder 5 is approaching, since the leftover has to be shared too.","7 / 4 = 7/4 = 1 and 3/4 bags for each bed. Each bed gets one whole bag and three quarters of another. Partial: 7/4 right with no mixed number given is approaching."]}]},{id:21,title:"In the Watering Can",short:"Tiny Bits",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model of particles too small to be seen to explain what happens to matter",html:()=>`<p class="big">A scoop of blue plant food goes into a watering can. Within a minute the water is a faint even blue with no grains left at the bottom, and every bed watered from the can gets the same dose.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wateringCan",84)}${F.icon("drop",84)}${F.icon("sprout",84)}</div>`,qs:["No grains are left and the color is even everywhere. Explain that using particles too small to see.","Why does an even mix matter to the gardener pouring from the can?","Walking past the beds you can smell the cut grass from twenty paces. Explain how that smell crosses twenty paces of air."],key:["The grains broke apart into particles far too small to see and spread out evenly among the water particles, so the plant food is everywhere in the can at the same concentration. (5-PS1-1)","Because every bed then gets the same dose. If the plant food sat in one spot, the first bed would get too much and the last none, even though the gardener poured the same amount. (5-PS1-1)","Particles of the smell substances left the cut grass and spread out through the air, moving between the air particles until enough reached your nose. They are matter, just far too small to see. (5-PS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying materials from measured properties rather than from appearance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four soil samples came from different corners of the garden. The same dry amount of each was tested.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Sample</th><th>Mass of 100 ml, dry</th><th>Holds its shape when rolled wet?</th><th>Water poured through drains in</th><th>Floats when dropped in water?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>150 g</td><td>no, falls apart</td><td>8 seconds</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>130 g</td><td>yes, into a firm ring</td><td>over 5 minutes</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>110 g</td><td>yes, but it crumbles</td><td>50 seconds</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>20 g</td><td>no, springs back</td><td>30 seconds</td><td>yes, at first</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The four samples are sand, clay, loam, and peat.</p>`,qs:["Which sample is the clay? Name two results that point to it.","Which sample is the sand, and which result is the clearest giveaway?","Sample D is the peat. Which measurement makes that obvious, and why would a gardener dig peat-like material into a sandy bed?"],key:["B. It rolls into a firm ring and takes over five minutes to drain, both of which fit clay with its very fine particles packed close together. (5-PS1-3)","A. Water drained through it in 8 seconds, far faster than any other, which is what coarse sand grains with big gaps between them do. (5-PS1-3)","Its mass, 20 g for the same 100 ml, is a fraction of the others, because it is mostly light plant material and air. A gardener digs it in because it holds water and air in a bed that would otherwise drain too fast. (5-PS1-3)"]}]},{id:22,title:"The Sealed Terrarium",short:"Weigh In",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"using measurements to show that total weight is conserved through heating, cooling, and mixing",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class sealed three jars in March and weighed them every week until May. No jar was ever opened.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Sealed jar</th><th>What was inside</th><th>March</th><th>May</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>damp soil and two seedlings, which grew to three times their size</td><td>655 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>water left on a sunny sill, with drops on the glass and a smaller pool</td><td>390 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>water with garden lime stirred in until the water went cloudy</td><td>508 g</td><td>508 g</td></tr></table>`,qs:["The seedlings in jar 1 tripled in size. What does the jar weigh in May? Explain.","What does jar 2 weigh in May, and where is the missing pool water?","A student says jar 1 must be heavier, because plants were built out of nothing. Correct that."],key:["655 g. The plants built themselves out of the carbon dioxide, water, and minerals already inside the sealed jar, so matter was rearranged, not added. (5-PS1-2)","390 g. The water that left the pool is on the glass as drops and in the air inside the jar as vapor, all still sealed in. (5-PS1-2)","Nothing was built out of nothing. The new plant material came from gas, water, and minerals that were already in the sealed jar, so the total weight is exactly the same and the jar's contents have simply been rearranged. (5-PS1-2)"]},{std:["5-PS1-4"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding from evidence whether mixing two substances made a new substance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three mixings tried in the potting shed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Mixing</th><th>What was seen</th></tr>
     <tr><td>a copper coin in vinegar with a pinch of salt, left two days</td><td>a blue green crust on the coin that will not rinse off, and the vinegar turned faintly blue</td></tr>
     <tr><td>garden sand and dry seeds, poured together</td><td>a sieve separated every seed back out whole and the sand was unchanged</td></tr>
     <tr><td>baking powder stirred into warm water</td><td>bubbles rose for about a minute, then nothing; the water tasted faintly salty and no powder was left</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which two mixings made a new substance? Give the evidence for each.","Which one is only a mixture, and what is the evidence?","Rinsing would not remove the blue green crust. Explain why that single fact is such strong evidence."],key:["The coin in vinegar and salt, because a blue green substance formed that cannot be rinsed off and the liquid changed color, and the baking powder in water, because a gas bubbled off and the powder was used up. (5-PS1-4)","The sand and seeds. A sieve returned every seed whole and the sand was unchanged, so they were only mixed. (5-PS1-4)","Because rinsing removes a substance that is merely sitting on a surface. A coating that will not rinse off is chemically joined to the metal, which means it is a new substance rather than something stuck on. (5-PS1-4)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Lettuce in Water",short:"Plant Food",parts:[{std:["5-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from measurements that plants get their material for growth from air and water",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two trays of lettuce were grown side by side on the classroom bench. One sat in weighed compost, the other had its roots in plain water with a measured trace of minerals and no soil at all.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("herb",80)}${F.icon("drop",80)}${F.icon("sun",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Tray in compost</th><th>Tray in water</th></tr>
     <tr><td>plants, dried, at the start</td><td>3 g</td><td>3 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>plants, dried, after five weeks</td><td>96 g</td><td>91 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>compost or minerals lost</td><td>4 g of compost</td><td>3 g of minerals</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Both trays gained about the same dry weight. What does that tell you about whether soil is needed?","The compost tray gained 93 g and lost only 4 g of compost. Where did the rest of the material come from?","Someone looks at this and says soil is useless to a plant. Give two reasons that goes too far."],key:["Soil is not where the material comes from. A tray with no soil at all grew almost as much dry material as the one in compost. (5-LS1-1)","From air and water. Carbon dioxide taken in through the leaves and water taken up by the roots are built into the plant using energy from sunlight. (5-LS1-1)","Soil supplies the small amounts of minerals a plant needs, which is why the water tray needed minerals added, and it holds the plant up and holds water and air around the roots. It is not the source of the bulk of the material, which is different from being useless. (5-LS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Look at the chain. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model to trace the energy in an animal's food back to the sun",html:()=>`<p class="big">One path energy takes through the spring garden.</p>
     <p class="huge" style="font-size:2.2rem;margin:10px 0;text-align:center">${F.icon("sun",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("leaf",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("caterpillar",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("bluebird",46)}</p>
     <p class="big">Caterpillars eat the new leaves. A bird feeds the caterpillars to its chicks.</p>`,qs:["Trace the energy in a chick's meal back to its source.","A bird brings hundreds of caterpillars to the nest over two weeks. Explain why so many are needed.","The garden is covered over for a week so no sunlight reaches it. Predict what happens along this chain and explain why."],key:["From the caterpillar, which got it from the new leaves, which captured energy from sunlight and stored it in the food they made. (5-PS3-1)","Most of the energy in each caterpillar was already used up by the caterpillar itself for its own living and growing, and the chick uses most of what is left for growing, moving, and keeping warm. Only a small part of each meal ends up as new chick, so a great many are needed. (5-PS3-1)","The leaves cannot make food, so they stop growing and start to fail, the caterpillars have less to eat and fewer survive, and the bird has less to bring to the nest. Taking away the sunlight starves the whole chain, because every step depends on it. (5-PS3-1)"]}]},{id:24,title:"The Spring Bed",short:"Matter Cycle",parts:[{std:["5-LS2-1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Look at the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with a model how matter moves among plants, animals, decomposers, and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">A model of one garden bed. Clover was grown over the winter and dug in before planting.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>How</th></tr>
     <tr><td>air and soil</td><td>clover and vegetables</td><td>taken in through leaves and roots; clover roots also host bacteria that capture nitrogen from the air</td></tr>
     <tr><td>plants</td><td>slugs, caterpillars, and people</td><td>eaten</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dug-in clover, roots, leaves, and waste</td><td>worms, fungi, and bacteria in the soil</td><td>broken down</td></tr>
     <tr><td>worms, fungi, and bacteria</td><td>air and soil</td><td>minerals and gases released</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("herb",70)}${F.icon("caterpillar",70)}${F.icon("mushroom",70)}${F.icon("snail",70)}</div>`,qs:["Clover roots host bacteria that capture nitrogen from the air. Explain why a gardener would grow clover and then dig it in rather than harvest it.","Name the decomposers in this model and say what they do.","The gardener sweeps every leaf and stalk off the bed and burns it. Predict two effects on this model.","Follow one nitrogen atom from the air into a lettuce leaf using this model."],key:["The nitrogen captured by the bacteria is in the clover plant. Digging it in hands that nitrogen to the decomposers, who release it into the soil where the next crop's roots can take it up. Harvesting the clover would carry the nitrogen away. (5-LS2-1)","Worms, fungi, and bacteria in the soil. They break down dead plant material and waste into simpler substances that plants can take up again. (5-LS2-1)","The minerals in that material leave the bed instead of returning to it, so the soil gets poorer and the gardener has to bring in fertilizer, and the decomposers lose their food supply so there are fewer of them to work the soil. (5-LS2-1)","Bacteria on the clover roots take nitrogen from the air and fix it into the clover, the clover is dug in, decomposers break it down and release the nitrogen into the soil, and a lettuce root takes it up and builds it into a leaf. (5-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Up and Down in the Garden",short:"Down",parts:[{std:["5-PS2-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the gravitational force Earth exerts on objects is directed down",html:()=>`<p class="big">Water poured from a can falls to the bed. A string with a stone on the end hangs beside a new bean trellis so it can be set upright.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wateringCan",80)}${F.icon("sprout",80)}${F.icon("drop",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which way does the poured water travel, and which way does the stone on the string point?","A bean shoot grows upward even in the dark, away from the pull. Does that mean the pull is not acting on it? Explain.","A seedling tray is tipped on its side for a week. The shoots bend and keep growing upward. What is the plant sensing, and which direction is it moving away from?"],key:["Both go straight down toward the ground. (5-PS2-1)","No. The pull is still acting on it. The plant senses which way down is and grows its shoot the opposite way on purpose, using energy to do it. Growing against a force does not mean the force has gone. (5-PS2-1)","It is sensing the direction of Earth's pull, which is down, and it grows its shoot in the opposite direction, which is why the shoots bend upward even though the tray turned. (5-PS2-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the story. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with an example how the geosphere, biosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere interact",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Earth system has four parts. The geosphere is rock and soil. The biosphere is living things. The hydrosphere is all the water. The atmosphere is air.</p>
     <p class="big">A spring downpour hits a bare allotment. Water runs across the surface and carries topsoil into the ditch, which runs brown for a day. On the neighboring plot, where clover was left growing all winter, the rain soaks in and the ditch beside it stays clear. Two days later earthworms are working near the surface of the planted plot and nowhere on the bare one.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("rain",72)}${F.icon("herb",72)}${F.icon("snail",72)}${F.icon("mountain",72)}</div>`,qs:["Name the two spheres interacting when rain carries topsoil into the ditch.","Clover was growing on one plot and the ditch beside it stayed clear. Explain that as an interaction between spheres.","Earthworms appear on the planted plot and not the bare one. Which spheres are connected there, and in which direction does the effect run?"],key:["The hydrosphere and the geosphere. Water moving across the land picks up soil and carries it somewhere else, which is erosion. The atmosphere delivered the rain. (5-ESS2-1)","Living clover, the biosphere, held the geosphere in place with its roots and slowed the rain with its leaves, so the hydrosphere soaked in rather than running off with soil in it. (5-ESS2-1)","The biosphere and the geosphere. The clover and the moisture it helped the soil hold changed the soil conditions, which allowed worms to live near the surface, and the worms then change the soil again by opening channels through it, so the effect runs both ways. (5-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Spring Sky",short:"Sky",parts:[{std:["5-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the sun looks brightest because of how near it is, not because it gives off the most light",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three stars easily seen in spring, with the light each gives off and its distance.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Star</th><th>Light it gives off</th><th>Distance from Earth</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>1 unit</td><td>8 light-minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Arcturus</td><td>about 170 units</td><td>37 light-years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Spica</td><td>about 2,000 units</td><td>250 light-years</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sun",76)}${F.icon("star",76)}${F.icon("telescope",76)}</div>`,qs:["Rank the three by light given off, then by how bright they look from the garden. What do you notice?","Spica gives off more than ten times the light of Arcturus but looks fainter. Explain using the distance column.","A gardener says the stars go out during the day. Correct that, and explain what is really happening."],key:["By light given off: Spica, then Arcturus, then the sun. By how bright they look: the sun by an enormous margin, then Arcturus, then Spica. The order is almost reversed, because of distance. (5-ESS1-1)","Spica is 250 light-years away and Arcturus is 37, so Spica is nearly seven times further. Light spreads out as it travels, and that difference in distance outweighs the difference in light given off. (5-ESS1-1)","They do not go out. They are still shining, but sunlight scattering in our atmosphere makes the daytime sky far brighter than the faint light arriving from them, so we cannot pick them out. (5-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding patterns in graphed data about shadows, day length, and the night sky",html:()=>`<p class="big">A 1 m stake in the bed had its noon shadow measured on the twenty-first of four months, and the sunrise direction was noted.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Noon shadow</th><th>Hours of daylight</th><th>Sun rose</th></tr>
     <tr><td>21 December</td><td>2.20 m</td><td>9.2</td><td>south of east</td></tr>
     <tr><td>21 March</td><td>1.00 m</td><td>12.1</td><td>due east</td></tr>
     <tr><td>21 June</td><td>0.40 m</td><td>15.0</td><td>north of east</td></tr>
     <tr><td>21 September</td><td>1.00 m</td><td>12.2</td><td>due east</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern across the four dates, using both the shadow and the daylight columns.","The sun rises due east on only two of these dates. What is special about those two dates?","Spring brings a different set of stars overhead at 10 p.m. than winter did. Explain why."],key:["As the daylight hours rise the noon shadow shortens, and they move together. The extremes are December, with the longest shadow and shortest day, and June, with the shortest shadow and longest day, and the two 1.00 m dates sit exactly between. (5-ESS1-2)","They are the equinoxes, when day and night are about equal everywhere. On those two dates the sun rises due east and sets due west, and the daylight hours in the chart are both about 12. (5-ESS1-2)","Earth travels around the sun over the year, so at the same hour of night in spring we face a different part of space than in winter, and a different set of stars is overhead. (5-ESS1-2)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Water for Growing",short:"Water",parts:[{std:["5-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the amounts and percentages of water and fresh water in Earth's reservoirs",html:()=>`<p class="big">Where Earth's water is, and what the fresh part of it gets used for.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>All of Earth's water</th><th>Share</th></tr>
     <tr><td>salty ocean</td><td>about 97%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>frozen in ice and glaciers</td><td>about 2%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>groundwater, lakes, rivers, soil, air</td><td>about 1%</td></tr></table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>How people use the fresh water they take</th><th>Rough share</th></tr>
     <tr><td>growing food</td><td>about 70%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>industry</td><td>about 20%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>homes</td><td>about 10%</td></tr></table>`,qs:["About what percentage of all Earth's water is fresh and liquid? Which row of the first chart is it?","Growing food takes about 70% of the fresh water people use. Why would farming need so much more than homes?","A town wants to cut its water use by a quarter. Using the second chart, where should it look first, and why?"],key:["About 1%, which is the third row, groundwater, lakes, rivers, soil, and air together. The 2% in ice is fresh but frozen. (5-ESS2-2)","Every crop takes up and loses enormous amounts of water as it grows, far more than a person drinks or washes with, and fields cover huge areas. Food is where almost all the water goes. (5-ESS2-2)","At how food is grown and watered, since that is about 70% of the use. Cutting household use by a quarter would barely change the total, because homes are only about 10% to start with. (5-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["5-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two reports. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information about ways communities use science to protect resources and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two towns tried to help the insects that pollinate crops and gardens.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Town</th><th>What they did</th><th>Result after two years</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Fieldbrook</td><td>left a three meter strip of wildflowers along every council verge and mowed it only once, in late autumn</td><td>four times as many bee and hoverfly species counted, and a lower mowing bill</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Greenway</td><td>asked growers to plant a flowering hedge along one side of each large field</td><td>fruit set in the nearby rows improved, and less soil blew off the field edges</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("bee",70)}${F.icon("purpleFlower",70)}${F.icon("butterfly",70)}</div>`,qs:["Mowing once in late autumn instead of often gave four times as many species. Explain why the timing matters.","Greenway's hedge improved fruit set and also held soil. Explain each of those two effects.","Combine the two reports into advice for a third town with bare verges and large open fields."],key:["Flowers need time to bloom and set seed, and insects need somewhere to feed and shelter through the season. Frequent mowing removes the flowers before they can do either. Cutting once at the end of the season lets the whole cycle finish. (5-ESS3-1)","The hedge gives pollinating insects food and shelter close to the crop, so more flowers get pollinated and more fruit sets. It also slows the wind at the field edge, so less soil is picked up and blown away. (5-ESS3-1)","Leave wide wildflower strips on the verges and mow them only once in late autumn, and plant flowering hedges along the field edges. The advice has to draw on each report. (5-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Design a Seed Tray",short:"Design It",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Listen, then answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",read:"The school garden club starts its seeds in trays on the classroom windowsill. Over spring break nobody is in the building, and last year most of the seedlings dried out and died in five days. The club wants something that keeps the trays watered for seven days with nobody there. It must sit on a windowsill forty centimeters deep, it must not leak onto the sill, and it has to be built from bottles, string, and containers brought from home, because the club has no budget.",qs:["Say the problem in one sentence.","Name three things the finished design must do to count as a success.","Name two limits the club has to work inside."],key:["Seedlings dry out and die over the week-long break when nobody is there to water them, so the club needs a way to keep the trays watered for seven days unattended. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any three criteria from the story: keeps the trays watered for seven days with nobody there, fits a windowsill forty centimeters deep, and does not leak onto the sill. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two constraints: it must fit a forty centimeter deep sill, and it must be made from bottles, string, and containers brought from home with no money spent. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing possible solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs, each left for seven days with the same trays of seedlings.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Seedlings alive after 7 days</th><th>Depth it needs</th><th>Leaked on the sill?</th><th>Free materials only?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>H: trays sitting in a shallow reservoir of water</td><td>31 of 40</td><td>35 cm</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>J: a bottle of water with cotton string wicks running into each tray</td><td>38 of 40</td><td>38 cm</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>K: a bought timer and drip irrigation kit</td><td>40 of 40</td><td>30 cm</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design kept the most seedlings alive? Explain why it is still not the choice here.","Compare H and J. Which better meets the criteria, and what in the design explains the difference?","Which design would you recommend, and list every criterion and limit it satisfies."],key:["K kept all 40 alive, but it has to be bought, and the club has no budget, so it breaks the free materials limit. (3-5-ETS1-2)","J, with 38 of 40 against 31. The string wicks draw water up steadily as the compost dries, so each tray gets water through the whole week, while a shallow reservoir runs out or leaves the top of the compost dry. (3-5-ETS1-2)","J. It kept 38 of 40 seedlings alive for seven days unattended, needs 38 cm which fits the 40 cm sill, did not leak, and is made from a bottle and string brought from home. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the test plan. Answer out loud.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming the failure points to improve",html:()=>`<p class="big">The club tested how many string wicks each tray needed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Trial 1</th><th>Trial 2</th></tr>
     <tr><td>wicks per tray</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottle of water</td><td>2 liters</td><td>2 liters</td></tr>
     <tr><td>trays and compost</td><td>4 trays, same compost, same amount</td><td>4 trays, same compost, same amount</td></tr>
     <tr><td>days left alone</td><td>7</td><td>7</td></tr>
     <tr><td>seedlings alive after</td><td>22 of 40</td><td>38 of 40</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what went wrong</td><td>the far corners of each tray stayed bone dry</td><td>the bottle ran empty on day 6</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What was held the same, and what was changed? Why does that make the test fair?","Suppose trial 2 had also used a 4 liter bottle. Could you still say the extra wicks made the difference? Explain.","Name one change that would fix the failure in each trial."],key:["The bottle size, the trays, the compost and its amount, and the seven days were all held the same, and only the number of wicks changed, so the difference in survival can be traced to the wicks. (3-5-ETS1-3)","No. Two things would have changed, the wicks and the water supply, so you could not tell which one did it. That is not a fair test. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Trial 1 left corners dry, so add more wicks or spread the wick ends across the tray rather than at one spot. Trial 2 ran dry on day 6, so use a larger bottle, add a second bottle, or run slightly fewer wicks so the supply lasts the full week. Any fix tied to the stated failure counts. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:29,title:"A Year in Two Regions",short:"The Year",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.1"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing how pre-Columbian nations organized a year around the land they lived on",html:()=>`<p class="big">Many nations organized the year as a round of seasonal work. Here are two, side by side.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Season</th><th>A Pacific Northwest coast nation</th><th>A Great Plains nation</th></tr>
     <tr><td>spring</td><td>first salmon run; gathering shoots and roots</td><td>bands gather after winter; new grass brings the herds back</td></tr>
     <tr><td>summer</td><td>fishing, sea mammal hunting, berry picking, trading up and down the coast</td><td>following the herds; large gatherings of many bands</td></tr>
     <tr><td>autumn</td><td>the big salmon run; smoking and drying the catch for winter</td><td>the main hunt; drying meat and preparing hides for winter</td></tr>
     <tr><td>winter</td><td>living on stored food in plank houses; carving, feasting, and storytelling</td><td>bands split into sheltered valleys and live on stored food</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Both nations did their biggest food work in autumn. Explain why autumn rather than spring.","In winter both nations lived mainly on stored food. Name one thing each did with the time that winter freed up.","The Plains bands came together in summer and split up in winter. Explain why the seasons pushed them in opposite directions.","Pick one row and name the biggest difference between what the two nations were doing that season."],key:["Autumn is when the food is at its peak and about to be gone: the big salmon run and the fattest herds. It is also the last chance to preserve food before nothing is available, so the work has to be done then. Partial: naming the peak of the food with nothing said about the last chance to preserve it is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","The coast nation carved, held feasts, and told the stories that carried their history. Plains bands sheltered in valleys, repaired and made equipment, and passed on stories too. Any answer drawn from the chart. Partial: one of the two nations answered is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","In summer the herds were large and out on open grassland, which suited many bands hunting and meeting together. In winter food was scarce and shelter mattered more, so smaller groups in separate sheltered valleys could survive where one large camp could not. Partial: one of the two seasons explained is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Any supported pair. For example in summer the coast nation was fishing and trading along a shoreline while the Plains nation was moving across grassland following herds. Partial: a row named with no difference between the two nations is approaching. (HSS-5.1)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Reading an Explorer's Route",short:"Routes",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.2"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the route notes. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing an explorer's route using directions and landmarks",html:()=>`<p class="big">A logbook records a voyage in stages. Follow it with a map in your head.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What the log says</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>left a Spanish port and sailed southwest to islands off the coast of Africa to take on water</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>held a course due west for five weeks across open ocean</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>made land at an island, then explored south and west among more islands</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>sailed northeast on the return, picking up winds that carried the ships back to Europe</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("map",76)}${F.icon("compass",76)}${F.icon("sailboat",76)}</div>`,qs:["Name the two directions the outbound voyage used, in order, and the direction of the return.","The ships stopped at islands off Africa before crossing. Why would an outbound voyage do that?","The outbound leg went west and the return went northeast, on a different line. What does that suggest the sailors had learned?","Whose voyage do these four stages describe, and which year?"],key:["Southwest first, then due west. The return was northeast. Partial: the outbound legs right with the return missing is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","To take on fresh water and food for a crossing with no land in between, and to pick up the winds that blow westward from that latitude. Partial: one of the supplies and the winds named is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","That the winds blow in different directions at different latitudes, so you go out on one wind belt and come home on another. Sailing back along the outbound line would have meant fighting the wind the whole way. Partial: noting the different line with nothing said about wind belts is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Columbus, in 1492. The Spanish port, the stop at the Canary Islands, the five week westward crossing, and landfall among Caribbean islands all fit that first voyage. Partial: the voyage named with no year, or the year with no reason from the stages, is approaching. (HSS-5.2)"]},{std:["HSS-5.3"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the causes of conflict over land between American Indian nations and settlers",html:()=>`<p class="big">Conflicts over land followed a pattern that repeated in region after region.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>settlers arrive in small numbers and are welcomed or tolerated; trade begins</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>more settlers arrive, clear forest, fence fields, and let livestock graze</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>fences and farms cut across hunting grounds and gathering places that a nation had always used</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>a nation protests; an agreement is made; more settlers arrive and the agreement is not kept</td></tr>
     <tr><td>5</td><td>open conflict, such as the war across New England in 1675 and 1676, after which the surviving nations lost most of their remaining land</td></tr>
   </table>`,qs:["Why would step 1 look like cooperation and step 3 look like conflict, when the settlers' aims had not changed?","Clearing forest and fencing fields is farming, not fighting. Explain how it still took something from a nation.","Step 4 happens over and over. Why would an agreement keep failing even when both sides signed it?","After the conflict in step 5 the surviving nations lost most of their remaining land. Explain who gained and who lost from that pattern overall."],key:["Numbers changed. A few traders take almost nothing and bring useful goods. Thousands of farmers need land, and the land they need is the land a nation is already using. Partial: saying the settlers wanted land with nothing said about the change in numbers is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","A nation depended on forest for hunting, on rivers for fishing, and on particular places for gathering food. Clearing and fencing them removed the food supply even though no one set out to fight. Partial: saying the land was taken with no account of the food supply is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Because the pressure behind it never stopped. Colonial leaders could sign in good faith and still not control the settlers who kept arriving, and some agreements were vague or deliberately stretched. An agreement that does not slow settlement does not hold. Partial: saying the settlers lied with nothing said about the continuing pressure is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Settlers and colonies gained land and the farms and towns built on it. American Indian nations lost their land, many of their people, and the ability to live as they had. Naming both sides plainly is the point. Partial: one side of the gain and the loss named is approaching. (HSS-5.3)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Printers and Second Thoughts",short:"Colonies",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.4"],n:4,dur:165,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the colonial press and the spread of news and ideas",html:()=>`<p class="big">By the 1760s nearly every colonial town of any size had a printer, and a printer did a great deal more than print.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>What a colonial printer produced</th><th>Who read it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>a weekly newspaper, often a single folded sheet</td><td>passed hand to hand and read aloud in taverns and shops, so one copy reached many people</td></tr>
     <tr><td>almanacs with weather, tides, planting dates, and sayings</td><td>bought by farming households, which often owned very few other books</td></tr>
     <tr><td>printed sermons and religious tracts</td><td>the largest single category of colonial printing, bought and read at home as well as heard</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pamphlets arguing a case</td><td>sold cheaply and reprinted by other printers in other colonies</td></tr>
     <tr><td>official notices, handbills, and advertisements</td><td>posted in public places</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">In 1735 a New York printer, John Peter Zenger, was tried for printing criticism of the royal governor. A jury refused to convict him.</p>
     <p class="big">In the 1730s and 1740s traveling preachers drew large outdoor crowds in a religious revival later called the Great Awakening. Printed sermons carried it from colony to colony. It split many congregations between those who welcomed the new preaching and those who stayed with their settled minister, and it got colonists used to judging an established authority for themselves.</p>`,qs:["One newspaper copy reached many people. Explain how, and why that mattered in a colony.","Why would an almanac be one of the few printed things in a farming household?","According to the note, what was the Great Awakening, and how did printing carry it from one colony to another?","The Zenger jury refused to convict. Why is that trial remembered?"],key:["It was passed from hand to hand and read aloud in taverns and shops, so people who could not read or could not afford a copy still heard the news. News spread far beyond the number of copies printed. Partial: saying people shared it with nothing said about reading aloud or reaching non-readers is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","It was genuinely useful every week: weather, tides, and planting dates affected the work, and it was cheap. A household with little money for books would still buy the one that helped the farm. Partial: saying it was cheap with no use named is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","A religious revival in the 1730s and 1740s in which traveling preachers drew large outdoor crowds, which split many congregations between those who welcomed the new preaching and those who stayed with their settled minister. Printing carried it because sermons were printed cheaply and reprinted by printers in other colonies, so an argument made in one colony could be read across all thirteen. Credit as well a student who adds that it got colonists used to judging an established authority for themselves. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so describing the revival with nothing on printing sits there. (HSS-5.4)","Because a jury declined to punish a printer for criticising a governor, which pushed the idea that printing the truth about officials should not be a crime. It is an early step toward freedom of the press. Partial: describing the verdict with no link to freedom of the press is approaching. (HSS-5.4)"]},{std:["HSS-5.5"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining why many colonists opposed independence, as part of the causes of the Revolution",html:()=>`<p class="big">Independence was not the obvious answer to most colonists. Historians estimate roughly a fifth stayed loyal to Britain, and many more tried to stay out of it altogether.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Reason for staying loyal</th><th>Who often held it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>breaking the law of the land was rebellion, and rebellion brought disorder</td><td>officials, judges, and those who valued order above all</td></tr>
     <tr><td>trade and a livelihood depended on the empire and its navy</td><td>merchants and shipowners</td></tr>
     <tr><td>family, faith community, and property ties to Britain</td><td>recent arrivals and many landowners</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Britain had offered freedom to some enslaved people who left owners in rebellion</td><td>thousands of enslaved people who took that offer</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two different reasons a colonist might stay loyal to Britain.","A merchant's business depended on the empire. Explain why independence looked like a risk to him.","The last row is a different kind of reason from the first three. Explain the difference.","What happened to many Loyalists after 1783?"],key:["Any two from the chart: rebellion meant disorder and breaking the law, trade and livelihood depended on the empire, family and property ties to Britain, or the promise of freedom for enslaved people who left rebel owners. Partial: one reason named is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","He sold into a protected imperial market and his ships were guarded by the British navy. Independence meant losing that market and that protection with nothing certain in its place. Partial: one of the market and the naval protection named is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","The first three are reasons to prefer the existing order. The last is a reason to prefer whichever side offered personal freedom, which is not loyalty to Britain so much as a choice between two sides neither of which was offering freedom as a principle. Partial: saying the last row is about enslaved people with no account of how the reason differs is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Tens of thousands left, many for Canada, some for Britain or the Caribbean, often losing their property. Those who stayed faced suspicion and in some places confiscation of what they owned. Partial: saying they left with nothing said about property or suspicion is approaching. (HSS-5.5)"]},{std:["HSS-5.6"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the end of the Revolution and the terms of the peace",html:()=>`<p class="big">The last major campaign and the treaty that followed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Event</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Yorktown, autumn 1781</td><td>a British army was pinned against the Virginia coast by American and French troops on land and a French fleet at sea, and surrendered</td></tr>
     <tr><td>talks in Paris, 1782 and 1783</td><td>American, British, French, and Spanish representatives negotiated separately and together</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Treaty of Paris, 1783: term one</td><td>Britain recognized the United States as independent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>term two</td><td>the new country's western boundary was set at the Mississippi River</td></tr>
     <tr><td>term three</td><td>Americans kept fishing rights off Newfoundland, and both sides made promises about debts and about Loyalists' property</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Explain why the British army at Yorktown could not simply withdraw.","Name the most important term of the treaty for the new country, and say why.","The treaty set the western boundary at the Mississippi River. Who was not at the talks but was living on that land?","Why would fishing rights be worth arguing about in a peace treaty?"],key:["It was trapped against the coast with armies in front of it and a French fleet behind, so it could not be supplied, reinforced, or taken off by sea. Surrender was the only option left. Partial: naming the armies in front with nothing said about the fleet behind is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Britain recognizing independence, because without that there was no country. Everything else in the treaty depended on it. Partial: the term named with no reason is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","The American Indian nations who lived between the mountains and the Mississippi. Their land was handed from one European claim to a new country in a treaty they were not party to and did not agree to. Partial: naming the nations with nothing said about their not being party to the treaty is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Because the cod fishery off Newfoundland was worth an enormous amount, and whole New England towns made their living from it. Losing access would have ruined them. Partial: saying fish were useful with no account of the value to New England towns is approaching. (HSS-5.6)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Why a Written Plan",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.7"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining why the founders chose a written constitution instead of a monarch or an unwritten tradition",html:()=>`<p class="big">After the war, some people suggested making George Washington a king. He refused, and the convention wrote a document instead.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>A monarch</th><th>A written constitution</th></tr>
     <tr><td>holds power for life and passes it to a child</td><td>offices are elected and limited, and the rules are the same for whoever holds them</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the rules are custom, and can be read differently as it suits</td><td>the rules are written down and anyone can read them</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a bad ruler can only be removed by force</td><td>officials can be voted out, impeached, or overruled by the courts</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what is allowed depends on who is in charge</td><td>what is allowed is the same for everyone, including the government</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two advantages of writing the rules down rather than relying on custom.","The chart says officials can be voted out, impeached, or overruled by the courts. Explain how each of those works as a different route.","Washington refused a crown and later left office after two terms. Why did that choice matter more than any speech he could have made?","A written constitution can still be ignored. What has to be true for a written document to actually hold power in check?"],key:["Anyone can read them and check what the government is allowed to do, and the same rules apply to whoever is in office, so they cannot be quietly reinterpreted to suit the person in charge. Partial: one advantage named is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Voting out is done by the people at an election. Impeachment is done by Congress, for serious wrongdoing in office. Overruling is done by the courts, by ruling that an action or law conflicts with the Constitution. Partial: two of the three routes explained is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Because it set a precedent in practice. Having held the most power in the country, he handed it over voluntarily, which showed that the office mattered more than the person and made it far harder for any successor to refuse to leave. Partial: saying he was modest with no account of the precedent is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Courts willing to enforce it, officials who accept rulings and election results, and a public that notices and objects when the rules are broken. A document only holds if people hold to it. Partial: one of the three conditions named is approaching. (HSS-5.7)"]},{std:["HSS-5.8"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how cotton and the spread of settlement westward were connected before 1860",html:()=>`<p class="big">After 1790 a machine that quickly separated cotton fiber from seed made growing cotton far more profitable, and the effects spread west.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Rough cotton crop</th><th>What else was happening</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1790</td><td>about 3,000 bales</td><td>cotton is a minor crop</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1820</td><td>about 335,000 bales</td><td>planters move west into Alabama and Mississippi for fresh land</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850</td><td>about 2,100,000 bales</td><td>cotton is the country's largest export, shipped to mills in Britain and New England</td></tr>
     <tr><td>through this period</td><td></td><td>about a million enslaved people were forcibly moved from the older southern states to the new cotton lands, often separated from their families</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe what happened to the cotton crop between 1790 and 1850.","Why did planters keep moving west instead of growing more cotton where they already were?","Cotton grown in the South was spun in mills in Britain and New England. Explain how that connected regions that were far apart.","The last row records what happened to about a million people. Say plainly what that was."],key:["It grew from about 3,000 bales to about 2,100,000, several hundred times over in sixty years. Partial: saying it grew a great deal with no figures from the chart is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Cotton wears out soil, and fresh western land produced far bigger crops. Cheap new land plus high cotton prices made moving west the profitable choice. Partial: naming the cheap land with nothing said about worn soil, or the reverse, is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Southern plantations grew the raw cotton, northern and British mills turned it into cloth, and ships, railroads, banks, and insurers in the North and in Britain all made money moving and financing it. An economy built on cotton reached far beyond where cotton grew. Partial: naming the mills with nothing said about the ships, banks or insurers is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","About a million enslaved people were forcibly moved hundreds of miles from the older southern states to the new cotton lands, sold away from husbands, wives, parents, and children, with no say and no way back. Partial: saying people were moved with nothing said about families being broken up is approaching. (HSS-5.8)"]},{std:["HSS-5.9"],n:5,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. A map is fine to look at.",desc:"locating Great Plains states and naming their capitals",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.icon("wheat",96)}${F.icon("map",96)}</div>
     <p class="big">Questions about the plains states.</p>`,qs:["Name four states usually counted as the Great Plains.","What is the capital of Kansas, and what is the capital of Nebraska?","Which plains state is the largest by area, and what is its capital?","Which state's border is formed on the east by the Missouri River and on the north by Canada?","How many states are there in all, and name two that border Canada and two that border Mexico."],key:["Any four of North Dakota, South Dakota, Nebraska, Kansas, Oklahoma, and Texas. Accept Montana, Wyoming, Colorado, and New Mexico, whose eastern parts are plains. Partial: two or three of the four named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Topeka, Kansas and Lincoln, Nebraska. Note if the student names Wichita or Omaha, which is the usual slip. Partial: one of the two capitals right is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Texas, capital Austin. Partial: the state named with no capital is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","North Dakota. Accept South Dakota if the student notes the Missouri runs through it rather than along its eastern edge, and explains. (HSS-5.9)","50 states. Canada: any two of Washington, Idaho, Montana, North Dakota, Minnesota, Michigan, Ohio, Pennsylvania, New York, Vermont, New Hampshire, Maine, and Alaska. Mexico: any two of California, Arizona, New Mexico, and Texas. Partial: the count right with one border answered is approaching. (HSS-5.9)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[2].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace(/__WC__/g,WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level passage read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Cite the Evidence",std:["RI.6.1"],needs:["RI.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite the exact words you are relying on.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two beds sit six feet apart in the same garden. One is sheltered by a fence on its windward side and one is in the open.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>Use the text to explain two separate ways the sheltered bed is likely to differ from the open one. Cite the evidence for each.</li><li>The text ends by saying none of this is a problem to be fixed. What does the writer want a gardener to do instead? Cite the evidence.</li></ol>${microHtml(true)}`,key:["Two differences, each with evidence. It loses less water from the leaves, and it is warmer at dawn: “A fence, a hedge, or a building slows the wind, and slower wind means less water lost from the leaves and less chill on a cold night,” and “The sheltered side of a fence is measurably warmer at dawn than the open ground beyond it.” (RI.6.1, citing textual evidence for an inference)","Match the plant to the spot rather than try to change the spot. Cites “A gardener who knows where the cold wet corner is plants the things that like it there,” and “Half of growing well is matching the plant to the pocket.” (RI.6.1)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: How Many Fit Inside",std:["6.NS.A.1"],needs:["5.NF.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the reciprocal you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>How many 1/8 cup scoops can be filled from 3/4 of a cup of seed?</li><li>2/3 ÷ 4/5</li><li>A bed 3 1/2 feet long is marked into sections 7/8 of a foot long. How many sections is that?</li></ol>`,key:["6 scoops. 3/4 ÷ 1/8 = 3/4 × 8/1 = 24/4 = 6. Check: 6 × 1/8 = 6/8 = 3/4. (6.NS.A.1)","5/6. Dividing by 4/5 means multiplying by 5/4, so 2/3 × 5/4 = 10/12 = 5/6. (6.NS.A.1)","4 sections. 3 1/2 = 7/2, and 7/2 ÷ 7/8 = 7/2 × 8/7 = 56/14 = 4. Check: 4 × 7/8 = 28/8 = 3 1/2. (6.NS.A.1, a word problem with fraction division)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Cite the Story",std:["RL.6.1"],needs:["RL.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite the exact words you are relying on.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The story never tells you what Thandi learned from the trial. Use evidence from the story to say what she now knows that nobody knew in the winter.</li><li>Mr. Ferraro changes his mind without ever saying so. Trace that change with three pieces of evidence, in the order they appear.</li></ol>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["She learned which bean suits which end of the bed, and that the ninety feet are three different growing places rather than one. Evidence: “Same seed, same day, same water,” which is what makes the comparison fair, and “Kentucky Wonder. It did better down here than any of the others.” (RL.6.1, citing evidence for an inference)","Three pieces in order: he predicts failure, “Half your rows will fail”; he inspects it at length without speaking, “walked the whole length of it twice without saying anything, which for him was a speech,” and concedes the point, “But you would not have known anything”; and then he copies the design, “the next morning Thandi found a row of stakes along his north edge, and a new string, and six small signs.” (RL.6.1, evidence traced across a text)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Beds That Are Not Rectangles",std:["6.G.A.1"],needs:["5.NF.B.4"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Write the formula you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A triangular corner bed has a base of 9 feet and a height of 4 feet. What is its area?</li><li>A bed shaped like a parallelogram has a base of 7 1/2 feet and a height of 4 feet. What is its area?</li><li>A bed shaped like a trapezoid has parallel sides of 10 feet and 6 feet, and the distance between them is 5 feet. What is its area?</li></ol>`,key:["18 square feet. Area of a triangle is 1/2 × base × height, so 1/2 × 9 × 4 = 18. (6.G.A.1)","30 square feet. Area of a parallelogram is base × height, so 7 1/2 × 4 = 30. The slanted side is not the height. (6.G.A.1)","40 square feet. Average the two parallel sides and multiply by the distance between them: (10 + 6) ÷ 2 = 8, and 8 × 5 = 40. (6.G.A.1)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Fractions of a Cube",std:["6.G.A.2"],needs:["5.MD.C.5"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A cold frame is 3 1/2 feet by 2 feet by 1 1/2 feet. What is its volume?</li><li>Pack that same cold frame with cubes that are 1/2 a foot on each edge. How many cubes fit along each edge, how many cubes fit in all, and what is each cube worth in cubic feet? Show that the two answers agree.</li><li>A seed tray is 1/2 a foot by 1/2 a foot by 1/4 of a foot. What is its volume?</li></ol>`,key:["10 1/2 cubic feet. 3 1/2 × 2 × 1 1/2 = 7/2 × 2 × 3/2 = 42/4 = 10 1/2. (6.G.A.2)","7 cubes along the 3 1/2 foot edge, 4 along the 2 foot edge, and 3 along the 1 1/2 foot edge, which is 7 × 4 × 3 = 84 cubes. Each half-foot cube is 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/8 of a cubic foot, so 84 × 1/8 = 84/8 = 10 1/2 cubic feet, the same answer as question 1. (6.G.A.2, packing with unit fraction cubes)","1/16 of a cubic foot. 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/4 = 1/16. (6.G.A.2)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Off the Edge of the Map",std:["6.NS.C.6"],needs:["5.G.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud. You can sketch on your paper.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The map is extended so that the ground west of the gate and south of the gate get negative numbers. In which quadrant does the point (−3, 5) sit?</li><li>A marker at (4, −2) is reflected across the x-axis. Where does it land?</li><li>Two markers sit at (−6, −1) and (6, −1). Each one is the reflection of the other across which axis? How do you know from the numbers alone?</li></ol>`,key:["The second quadrant, which is the upper left: x is negative and y is positive. (6.NS.C.6b)","(4, 2). Reflecting across the x-axis keeps the x-coordinate and flips the sign of the y-coordinate. (6.NS.C.6b, signs of coordinates under reflection)","Across the y-axis. The y-coordinates are identical and the x-coordinates are opposites, which is exactly what a reflection across the y-axis does. (6.NS.C.6b)"]},13:{id:"L13",title:"Level Up: Pronouns on Trial",std:["L.6.1"],needs:["L.5.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Fix the pronoun: Mr. Ferraro gave the six signs to Thandi and I.</li><li>Name the pronoun and say what kind it is: Thandi herself marked out all eighteen sections.</li><li>Fix the shift: If a gardener wants a fair trial, you have to plant the same seed in every zone.</li></ol>`,key:["Mr. Ferraro gave the six signs to Thandi and me. After the preposition to, the pronoun takes the objective case. Test it by dropping Thandi: “gave the signs to me.” (L.6.1a and L.6.1b, pronoun case)","herself, an intensive pronoun. It is not needed for the sentence to make sense; it is there to emphasize that Thandi did it rather than somebody else. (L.6.1b, intensive pronouns)","If a gardener wants a fair trial, he or she has to plant the same seed in every zone. Also correct: “If gardeners want a fair trial, they have to plant…” The sentence cannot start in the third person and switch to you. (L.6.1c, correcting inappropriate shifts in pronoun person)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RI.5.9","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"];const WRITE_STD=["W.5.1","W.5.2","W.5.3"];const MATH_STD=["5.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3","5.OA.A.1","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","5.G.A.1","6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.NS.C.6"];const PASSAGE_STD=READ_STD.concat(["W.5.8","W.5.2"]);const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0],pvVal="2.682";function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const PV_COLS=["Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones","Tenths","Hundredths","Thousandths"];function pvTable(){const raw=pvVal.replace(/[^0-9.]/g,""),dot=raw.indexOf(".");const whole=(dot<0?raw:raw.slice(0,dot)).slice(-4),frac=(dot<0?"":raw.slice(dot+1)).slice(0,3);let h="<tr>";PV_COLS.forEach(c=>h+=`<th>${c}</th>`);h+="</tr><tr>";for(let i=0;i<4;i++){const k=whole.length-(4-i);h+=`<td>${k>=0?whole[k]:""}</td>`}for(let i=0;i<3;i++)h+=`<td>${i<frac.length?frac[i]:""}</td>`;return h+"</tr>"}function pvHtml(){return`<label class="pv-in">Type a number<input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="pvInput" value="${pvVal}" autocomplete="off"></label><div class="tool-scroll"><table class="pv" id="pvTable">${pvTable()}</table></div><p class="small">Each place to the left is worth 10 times as much. Each place to the right is worth one tenth as much.</p>`}function strips(){const dens=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12],W=600,h=30;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+8} ${dens.length*(h+6)+4}" width="100%" style="max-width:640px" role="img" aria-label="Fraction strips for halves, thirds, fourths, fifths, sixths, eighths, tenths, and twelfths">`;dens.forEach((d,r)=>{for(let k=0;k<d;k++){const x=4+k*W/d,y=2+r*(h+6);s+=`<rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${W/d}" height="${h}" fill="${r%2?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="${x+W/d/2}" y="${y+h/2}" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="${d>8?12:16}" fill="var(--ink)">${d===1?"1":"1/"+d}</text>`}});return s+"</svg>"}function fracCard(){return`<ul class="starters"><li>To add or subtract: rewrite both fractions over the same bottom number, then add or subtract only the tops.</li><li>A quick common denominator is the two bottom numbers multiplied together. It is not always the smallest one.</li><li>Mixed number to improper: 2 2/3 = (2 × 3 + 2)/3 = 8/3.</li><li>To multiply: multiply the tops, then multiply the bottoms. A whole number can be written over 1.</li><li>“Of” means multiply. 2/5 of 3/4 is 2/5 × 3/4.</li><li>Benchmarks: compare each fraction with 0, 1/2, and 1 before you compute, then check your answer against the estimate.</li></ul>`}function volCard(){return F.table(["What to find","How"],[["Volume of a box","length × width × height, answer in cubic units"],["Volume from the base","base area × height, written V = B × h"],["A missing height","volume ÷ the base area"],["A solid made of two boxes","find each volume, then add them"],["Area of a triangle","1/2 × base × height"],["Area of a parallelogram","base × height"],["Area of a trapezoid","(base 1 + base 2) ÷ 2 × height"]])+`<p class="small">Volume counts the unit cubes that fill a solid with no gaps and no overlaps, and the answer is always in cubic units.</p>`}function gridCard(){return`<ul class="starters"><li>The horizontal line is the x-axis. The vertical line is the y-axis. They meet at the origin, (0, 0).</li><li>An ordered pair is always (x, y): travel along the x-axis first, then up parallel to the y-axis.</li><li>(3, 7) and (7, 3) are different points, because the order of the numbers is part of the name.</li><li>Points with the same first number sit on one vertical line. Points with the same second number sit on one horizontal line.</li></ul>`}function figsCard(){return`<ul class="starters"><li>A simile compares two things using like or as: “dried out like a cracker.”</li><li>A metaphor says one thing is another thing, with no like or as: “a thin green fuzz.”</li><li>Ask three questions: what two things are being compared, what do they have in common, and what does the comparison make you see or feel?</li><li>Then say what the phrase means in plain words.</li></ul>`}const STARTERS={"W.5.1":["The committee should keep ____ because ____.","My first reason is ____. The fact box says ____.","In addition, ____.","Someone might say ____. On the other hand, ____.","For this reason, ____."],"W.5.2":["One garden can hold more than one climate because ____.","The first cause is ____. For example, ____.","As a result, ____.","A microclimate is ____.","In short, ____."],"W.5.3":["At first, ____.","Then ____.","“____,” ____ said.","By the time ____, ____.","In the end, ____."],"W.5.8":["Warmth: ____ (Source A)","Water and air: ____ (Source B)","Depth and light: ____ (Source C)","Sources: A. ____ by ____. B. ____ by ____. C. ____ by ____."],"SL.5.2":["The report was mainly about ____.","The committee found that ____.","They recommended ____ because ____.","The graph shows ____.","The biggest difference on the graph is ____."],"SL.5.4":["The cold frame should go ____.","My first reason is ____, because the table shows ____.","My second reason is ____.","Someone might say ____, but ____.","I am asking the committee to ____."],"RI.5.9":["Source A says ____.","Source B says ____.","Source C says ____.","Putting all three together, ____."],"RL.5.4":["The phrase “____” is a ____.","It compares ____ with ____.","It makes me picture ____.","In plain words it means ____."],cite:["The text says, “____.”","I know ____ because the text says, “____.”","The words “____” show that ____."]};function sentHtml(st){const p=st&&st.parts&&st.parts[0],a=p&&p.alt&&p.alt.key;const k=a==="opinion"?"W.5.1":a==="facts"?"W.5.2":a==="story"?"W.5.3":p&&p.std&&p.std[0]||"cite";return`<ul class="starters">${(STARTERS[k]||STARTERS.cite).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function chunkHtml(){return F.table(["Word part","What it means"],[["photo","light: photosynthesis"],["synthesis","putting together: photosynthesis"],["hort","garden: horticulture"],["per + ann","through the years: perennial"],["micro","small: microclimate"],["therm","heat: thermometer"],["meter","a measure: thermometer"],["dorm","sleep: dormancy"],["dict","say: unpredictable"],["-ation, -ion","turns a verb into a noun: germination, evaporation"],["-able","can be: unpredictable"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the prefix and the ending, read the root, then put the whole word back together.</p>`}function tenseCard(){return F.table(["Tense or pair","How to build it","Example"],[["Past perfect","had + the verb","The crew had already left."],["Present perfect","has or have + the verb","The committee has met twice."],["Future perfect","will have + the verb","By June we will have finished."],["Tense shift","keep one time all the way through","We planted and watered, not planted and water."],["Correlative pairs","not only / but also, either / or, neither / nor, both / and","Neither the beans nor the peas came up."]])}const TOOLS={chunk:{name:"Word part chart",std:["RF.5.3"],html:chunkHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},pv:{name:"Place value chart to thousandths",std:["5.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3"],html:pvHtml,bind:b=>{const inp=b.querySelector("#pvInput"),tb=b.querySelector("#pvTable");if(inp&&tb)inp.oninput=()=>{pvVal=inp.value.replace(/[^0-9.]/g,"");tb.innerHTML=pvTable()}}},strips:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],html:strips},fcard:{name:"Fraction card",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],html:fracCard},vol:{name:"Volume and area card",std:["5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2"],html:volCard},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid card",std:["5.G.A.1","6.NS.C.6"],html:gridCard},figs:{name:"Simile and metaphor card",std:["RL.5.4"],html:figsCard},tense:{name:"Verb tense card",std:["L.5.1","L.6.1"],html:tenseCard},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.5.8","SL.5.2","SL.5.4","RI.5.9","RL.5.4","RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"]),html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"chunk",std:["RF.5.3"],msg:"The word part chart gives the meaning of the roots and affixes and uses words from the list as its examples, so count the word reading as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:MATH_STD,msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the products. Rate on whether the student set each problem up correctly and used the right unit, not on the arithmetic."},{t:"pv",std:["5.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The place value chart names the places and lines the digits up, so the student does not have to recall the place names. The ten times and one tenth reasoning still has to come from the student."},{t:"pv",std:["5.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The place value chart names the places and lines the digits up, which is most of what reading a decimal in words and comparing two of them takes. Count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.B.3"],msg:"Fraction strips let the student see a whole cut into equal shares, which is the model this stop would otherwise ask them to build. Rate on the division statement and on whether the leftover was shared, not on the picture."},{t:"fcard",std:["5.NF.B.3"],msg:"The fraction card gives the rules for mixed numbers and improper fractions. Count the conversion as supported and rate on whether the student read the fraction as a division in the first place."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"Fraction strips show the piece sizes lined up, so a student can read off a common denominator instead of reasoning to it. Rate on whether the student can write the equation and explain it."},{t:"fcard",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"The fraction card gives the rules for adding, multiplying, and converting mixed numbers, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recall."},{t:"vol",std:["5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2"],msg:"The card gives the volume formulas, the missing-height method, and the area formulas, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"grid",std:["5.G.A.1","6.NS.C.6"],msg:"The grid card names the axes and the origin and states the order of an ordered pair, so count the naming questions as supported and rate the travel and comparison answers."},{t:"figs",std:["RL.5.4"],msg:"The card defines simile and metaphor, so count the naming part of each answer as supported and rate the explanation the student gives."},{t:"tense",std:["L.5.1","L.6.1"],msg:"The verb tense card shows how to build each perfect tense and lists the correlative pairs, so count those items as supported."},{t:"sent",std:WRITE_STD,msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"The frames show how to label a note and lay out a source line, so count the source list as supported and rate whether the notes are in the student's own words."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.2","SL.5.4"],msg:"The frames supply the order of the talk, so count the sequencing check as supported and rate the content, the facts, and the pace."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.5.9","RL.5.4","RL.5.1","RI.5.1"],msg:"The frames remind the student to quote and to draw on each source. Rate on the accuracy of the quotation and the content of the answer."},{t:"passage",std:PASSAGE_STD,msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
